.] The display below lists all 16 equally likely ordered outcomes; 1/4 of the time we’ll pick a club first, and of these times, 1/4 will result in a diamond on the second pick, so 1/16 of the selections will have a club first and then a diamond:","position":{"start":{"line":14,"column":1},"end":{"line":14,"column":1}},"key":"Gce6V6ciOu"}],"key":"cmFB4lgIKu"},{"type":"code","lang":"","value":"SC  SD  SH  SS\nHC  HD  HH  HS\nDC  DD  DH  DS\nCC  CD  CH  CS   ← club first (4 of 16); CD is 1 of these 4","position":{"start":{"line":16,"column":1},"end":{"line":21,"column":1}},"key":"Vqfa7PDub9"}],"class":"tip","key":"ooCbslNll1"},{"type":"admonition","children":[{"type":"admonitionTitle","children":[{"type":"text","value":"Example: Picking Cards Without Replacement","position":{"start":{"line":24,"column":1},"end":{"line":24,"column":1}},"key":"AtrPrNgmZL"}],"key":"u4R7raAVjR"},{"type":"paragraph","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"children":[{"type":"text","value":"Suppose you pick two cards at random from four cards consisting of one of each suit: club, diamond, heart, and spade, without replacing the first card before the second card is picked. What is the probability of picking a club and then a diamond? The probability in this case is not ","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"key":"gi9L94oRjV"},{"type":"inlineMath","value":"1/4 \\times 1/4 = 1/16","position":{"start":{"line":27,"column":1},"end":{"line":27,"column":1}},"html":"<span class=\"katex\"><span class=\"katex-mathml\"><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><semantics><mrow><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>×</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">/</mi><mn>16</mn></mrow><annotation encoding=\"application/x-tex\">1/4 \\times 1/4 = 1/16</annotation></semantics></math></span><span class=\"katex-html\" aria-hidden=\"true\"><span class=\"b